@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for Linux (64-bit)
===========================================

Raspberry Pi: this needs the 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS. On the 32-bit one,
"Play @TITLE@" can't start (the system says it can't execute it, or that
the file isn't found). In a terminal, "getconf LONG_BIT" says 64 on a
64-bit system.

@FULL_TITLE@ (@STUDIO@), recompiled to run natively: the game's own
program translated into C++ and compiled for your computer, drawn with
Vulkan.

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the game: @RELEASE@,
as a disc image.


Your disc image
---------------
An image of your disc as it is: an .iso, .gcm or .ciso file. GameCube discs
aren't encrypted, so nothing has to be extracted first. Dolphin's
compressed formats aren't read: convert an .rvz, .wia or .gcz image in
Dolphin first (right-click the game in the list, Convert File, Format: ISO).
An NKit image has to be converted back to a full one with NKit.


Playing
-------
1. Unpack the download anywhere you can write to (your home folder is
   fine): double-click it in your file manager, or in a terminal
   "tar xzf @PACKAGE@-*.tar.gz". Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open the @PACKAGE@ folder and double-click "Play @TITLE@" (if your file
   manager asks, choose Execute or Run). From a terminal: ./"Play @TITLE@"
3. Click "Choose your disc image" and pick the file above, or drag it onto
   the window. (Where the desktop has no file chooser, type or paste the
   file's path.) It tells you straight away if it is the wrong game,
   another region or another revision.
4. Wait for the first build: a few minutes on a desktop PC. Your game
   files never leave your computer.
5. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs. "Add to the
   applications menu" puts @TITLE@ in your desktop's menu.

After that, Play @TITLE@ remembers your disc image (even in a new download
folder), and Play starts the game straight away. The game reads the image
while it runs, so leave it where it is (if you move it, choose it again).
After an update it builds the game again first, recompiling only what
changed.

The game's output is in work/run.log and work/run-errors.log, and the
build's in work/build.log; the game also keeps mkdd.log beside its settings
(below). Send them when reporting a problem.

Needs: a 64-bit Linux on x86-64 (the linux-x86_64 download) or ARM64 (the
linux-arm64 download) with glibc 2.35 or later (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12,
Fedora 36, Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm or later), and a graphics driver with
Vulkan 1.3: Mesa's Vulkan drivers (on Debian and Ubuntu, the
mesa-vulkan-drivers package) or NVIDIA's. Before building, Play @TITLE@
checks that there is one and says what to install if not. Updates use
curl (or wget) and tar, which nearly every Linux has.


Raspberry Pi
------------
- The 64-bit Raspberry Pi OS (see the top of this file), on a Raspberry Pi
  4 or 5 with 4 GB of memory or more. (The largest of the game's files
  takes about 1.5 GB to compile, so on a 2 GB Pi the first build leans on
  the swap file and is slower still.) Use the linux-arm64 download.
- Vulkan 1.3 for the Pi's graphics arrived with Mesa 24.3: Raspberry Pi OS
  Trixie (October 2025) or later has it. On an older Raspberry Pi OS, Play
  @TITLE@ says so before building: update Raspberry Pi OS.
- The first build compiles the whole game on the Pi itself, so it takes a
  while: much longer than on a desktop PC (an hour or more on a Pi 4), and
  a Pi with 4 GB or less compiles one file at a time. Leave it running.
  Later builds take compiled files from the cache.
- The game has not been measured on a Pi yet. In the settings menu, keep
  the render resolution at 1x, SMAA and FSR 1 off, vsync on, and play full
  screen (F11).
- "Add to the applications menu" (once the game is built) puts @TITLE@ in
  the Raspberry Pi menu, under Games.


Controls
--------
Four GameCube controllers. Controllers take the ports in the order each
first has a button pressed; the keyboard plays the first.

  GameCube controller   Xbox controller      Keyboard (player 1)
  A                     A (bottom)           X
  B                     X (left)             Z
  X                     B (right)            C
  Y                     Y (top)              S
  Z                     RB or LB             D
  L, R                  LT, RT               A, Space
  Start                 Start                Enter
  Control stick         left stick           arrows
  C-stick               right stick          I K J L
  D-pad                 D-pad                T G F H

Back + Start together, or F1 or Esc: the settings menu (the game pauses):
controls for each player (set by pressing them), the picture (size, render
resolution, a wider picture, SMAA, FSR 1, a CRT look, frame rate), full
screen, sound. F3: frame times. F9: capture frames for a bug report.
Alt+Enter or F11: full screen.

Settings, saves, captures and the game's log are kept in
~/.local/share/qilin/@GAME@ (or $XDG_DATA_HOME/qilin/@GAME@), with the disc
image you chose and the build's cache of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in here
--------------
Play @TITLE@        chooses your disc image, builds the game (once) and starts it
kit/qilin-disc      reads your disc image: takes @BOOT@ from it
kit/qilin           the recompiler: turns the disc's @BOOT@ into C++, and
                    says which disc an image is
kit/qilin-dsp       the same for the sound processor's program inside @BOOT@
kit/plan.tsv        which parts of the game are recompiled, and which run natively
kit/lib, include    the prebuilt rest of the game: its runtime, the disc
                    reader, the renderer, sound, the window and controls
                    (SDL3), the menu (Dear ImGui)
kit/toolchain/zig   zig, a C++ compiler (Clang, with its own libc++)
kit/icon.png        the menu entry's icon
kit/files.txt       every file this release holds (see below)
work/               made when building: the game (@EXE_LINUX@), built from your
                    disc, the recompiled code, the build's files and the logs
LICENSES/           the licences of everything included

zig keeps its own cache of compiled libraries in ~/.cache/zig.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/Qilin1066/MarioKartDD--QilinRecomp


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in Play @TITLE@ asks GitHub for a newer release of
@FULL_TITLE@ (it sends nothing about you or your computer). If there is one,
it downloads it, unpacks it beside this folder and swaps in the new kit and
launcher, then starts again and builds the game for the new version. If
anything fails on the way, the old version is left as it was. You can also
unpack a new download anywhere, even over this folder: it finds your disc
image and the cache by itself, and at its first start it removes files the
new kit no longer has (anything in kit/ or LICENSES/ that kit/files.txt
doesn't name). Your saves and settings are never in this folder, so nothing
of yours is touched.

The game is compiled for this computer's processor; moved to another
computer, it is built again. Compiled files come from the cache when
nothing they're made from has changed. A failed build leaves the working
game as it was; the one before a new build is kept as
work/@EXE_LINUX@.previous until the new one has run and closed normally.
"Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.

Before the game writes to a memory card, the save it replaces is kept: the
five most recent of each. The settings menu's Game page, Previous saves,
restores one and restarts (backing up the current save first).
